  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "eof-modes-and-pdo-index",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/eof-modes-and-pdo-index.md",
      "sha256": "3544f2f552ecd0f41c41eb09edf7012f49ac8421900f2b571b8498d37f2726b1",
      "reason": "Defines the PDO as the leading EOF with the global-mean anomaly removed, with area weighting, an arbitrary sign, explained variance that depends on grid, domain and record length, amplitude read from the regression on the standardised index, and an area-weighted pattern correlation between sources, all of which the question asks for.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build anomalies by removing the monthly climatology and the global-mean SST anomaly, and weight cells by sqrt(cos(latitude)). Fix the sign so the positive phase is cool in the central and western North Pacific, for every pattern and index, so a negative pattern correlation is a real disagreement. Put all four sources on one 1° grid before the EOF, because explained variance depends on grid and record length. Quote explained variance only across fields on the same grid and period.",
        "Report amplitude from the regression pattern (anomalies regressed on the standardised index, °C per standard deviation), not from the EOF. Its area-weighted RMS is the mode strength. Standardise the index to zero mean and unit variance. Use an area-weighted `analyze_correlation` (`weighting='area'`) for the pattern correlations with ERSSTv5."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "Each source's anomalies must be taken from its own 1950-2014 monthly climatology, which removes model bias before the index is built, and the baseline period fixes the anomaly and index definition. Events and transitions are judged on anomalies, with persistence.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The question specifies a 1950-2014 monthly climatology. Remove each field's own climatology (`compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology`), not the WOA23 one, so model bias cancels. State the baseline used. The published `pdo` index may sit on another baseline or construction, so offsets against it are not reconstruction errors.",
        "Define transitions by sign change of the 108-month running mean, as persistence-based events rather than single steps. State the merge rule, and that a crossing less than 3 years after the last one is merged into it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The pattern correlation between each model's regression pattern and the ERSSTv5 pattern is computed only over cells both have, with one grid and one mask, so the comparison needs a common mask.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The regression patterns come from analyses on each source's own domain, so do not apply a shared mask to them. Correlate each model pattern with the ERSSTv5 pattern over the cells the two share (`analyze_correlation` leaves out cells either one lacks). Compare the models on those correlations, and report the cell count."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The monthly index is strongly autocorrelated, so the lag-12 autocorrelation and the correlation with the published PDO need an effective sample size, and the caveat about what that correlation demonstrates (shared low-frequency variability, inflated by shared trends) must be stated.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Correlate the ERSSTv5-derived index with the published PDO as it is, without detrending, since these are two estimates of the same quantity. Quote the effective-sample-size-corrected significance (`analyze_correlation`) rather than the raw one. Monthly autocorrelation makes the number of independent samples far smaller than the number of months.",
        "Do not give a p-value for the spatial pattern correlations. Read the coefficient only."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The question requires bilinear regridding to 1°, which changes the land-sea mask. This is acceptable for maps and pattern comparisons, but the regridding choice must be stated.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "An EOF mode with its explained variance and regression pattern is grid-dependent, so put all sources on the common 1° grid before the EOF and take the numbers there. The question prescribes bilinear interpolation, so use `regrid_horizontal` with 'bilinear' and state the method. Fragment-wise, 'conservative' is the usual choice but the question's bilinear version is followed, and the answer must say so."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "CESM2 and MPI are native curvilinear (gn) while GFDL-ESM4 is already conservatively regridded (gr) to 1°, so the regridding steps and masks differ between the models.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 `tos` is already regridded (`gr`, 1x1 degree, conserve_order1) while CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are on native `gn` grids. Bilinear interpolation to 1° therefore affects them differently. The coarse MPI grid (250 km) cannot reproduce narrow features in amplitude."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "missing-values-and-land-cells",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "pitfalls/missing-values-and-land-cells.md",
      "sha256": "f576244f13dde06fed1777eaa9721c68844f61758f66fac704b44f1551e90df2",
      "reason": "Land cells carry the 1e20 fill value, which would dominate the EOF and area-weighted statistics if the masking is skipped when reading tos.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Land and missing cells carry 1e20, decoded to NaN by xarray. Use NaN-aware reductions with weights masked to the same valid cells. Any value near 1e20 in the output is an unmasked fill value. Build each field's valid mask from the field itself."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245.md",
      "sha256": "be5515ae7b8c8826c4e329bddcd5cbadc8e3e000292b7f6e83e1537af36a990c",
      "reason": "The historical runs have free internal variability that is not synchronised with observed years, so PDO phase timing and the index correlation with observations are not expected to match, which affects how the model comparison is interpreted.",
      "status": "dropped",
      "notes": [
        "Coupled historical runs have their own internal variability and only one member per model. Phase-transition years and index timing are not expected to match the observations, so compare statistics (variance explained, amplitude, autocorrelation, pattern), not individual years. State this caveat about the single member when judging the models."
      ]
    }
  ],
